\chapter{Informing implementation discussions}\label{chap:inform-impl}

\margintoc{}%
%
I started formalising \kl{Kernel CN} long after implementation had begun, and
benefited by the clarity of having an implementation and implementers I could
consult in moments of confusion.

However, this meant there were \emph{many} design decisions made quite
conservatively, because the implementation was defining the system along the
way, rather than using a well-understood pre-existing one. Syntax and
\kl{resource inference} were extended by the minimum required for verifying the
\kl{pKVM} \kl{buddy allocator}, lest performance suffer, rather than extended
based on a strong formal and holistic consideration of the constructs and
interactions at play.

As such, there are several restrictions in the implementation, which with the
benefit of hindsight and formalisation, are unnecessary, but persist
as technical debt. This chapter lists a few of these, and explains how the
formalisation brings much needed clarity to many questions around the
implementation.

% \url{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/labels/language}
% \url{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/labels/resource\%20reasoning}

\section{Supporting loads of partially initialised structs/unions}\label{sec:partial-init-structs}

So far, in verifying the \kl{buddy allocator}, writing a tutorial and working
with our industry partners, no code has loaded a partially (or completely)
uninitialised struct/union as a whole (loads of initialised members from a
struct which has other members uninitialised are fine).

Similar to uninitialised integer types, uninitialised primitive type members of
a struct/union only trigger \kl{UB} when they are used in certain ways. Such
loads only occur when whole structs/unions are used as rvalues, \eg{} in
assignments, function call arguments, function return values.

Remember, \kl{CN} lets one decompose ownership of a struct into ownership of
its fields, which means we can track the initialisation status of those
individually. This means that not supporting loads of partially initialised
structs/unions does not forfeit \kl{CN} an expressiveness.

The only valid situation which would need to be refactored to work around this
limitation is if partially initialised struct is passed to a function which
reads only the initialised fields. This is because the value would crosses a
function boundary. \kl{CN} checks each function against its own \kl{pre-} and
\kl{postconditions} and the \kl{specification} language has no syntax or
semantics to express that a field value itself is unspecified. In such
instances, the code could be refactored to only pass the initialised fields, or
a pointer to the struct with the appropriate precondition to only read the
initialised fields.

Although we wish to verify C code as is, because programmers need to change the
``code'' to write annotations anyway, such small, rare and easy to explain
refactors are a worthwhile trade-off if it makes the design and implementation
of \kl{CN} simpler. The book-keeping for tracking initialisation status in
symbolic terms, rather than in the name of a predicate, adds a non-trivial
amount of noise to the formalisation and implementation.

% Perhaps such an allowance simplifies data-flow analysis inside a compiler, so
% that it may safely ignore the values resulting from a load of partially
% uninitialised loads of a of struct, so long as none of the fields are accessed.
% It seems hard to think of a case for this allowance motivated by performance or
% programmer ergonomics, and so the experience from formalising this suggests
% that the current implementation of \kl{CN}, which does not allow loads from
% partially (including completely) uninitialised structs, can and will remain
% expressive enough for all of its use cases.

\section{Automatic unfolding scheme for logical functions}\label{sec:auto-unfold-functions}

One of the strange parts of working with \kl{CN} is that \kl(CN){predicate
definitions} can be (with a flag) \intro{automatically unfolded} but not logical
\kl(CN){functions}, even though both may be
recursive.\sidenote{\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/issues/483}{Cerberus\#483}.}
As such there is separate and cumbersome annotation to manually unfold a
\kl(CN){function}.

The way the implementation determines whether to unfold a \kl(CN){predicate} is
to always unfold it if there is no top-level if, or to check if the condition
or its negation is entailed before unfolding the corresponding branch.

However, thanks to the formalisation, we can see the implementation's strategy
conflates orthogonal considerations. In the formalisation
(\cref{fig:typing-res-pat}), including the \kl(CN){elaboration} rules
(\cref{fig:elab-fold}), (un)folding predicates does not require % chktex 36
calling the SMT solver, but determining which branch of an ordered-disjunction
to check does.

This suggests that it is fine to loosen the restrictions on branching in
\kl(CN){predicate definitions} (\S\ref{sec:restriction-branching}), and that the
approach of ``unfold until you reach a condition for which neither it nor its
negation are entailed'' would work for logical \kl(CN){functions} too.

Please note that with automatic constraint-based unfolding, it is
possible for users to \kl(CN){define predicates} which make \kl{CN}
diverge.\sidenote{\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/issues/451}{Cerberus\#451}.}
I propose a solution to this in~\fancyref{sec:pred-defn-checks}.

\section{Higher-order resources}

If we want \kl{CN} to be widely used in industry, it must support
concurrency.\sidenote{\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/issues/363}{Cerberus\#363}.}
We can use the formalisation to reflect on what changes would be needed to
support this.

Fractional permissions could be used to enable sharing. Beyond that, we have a
couple of options. Hard-coding support for locks as an extra kind of resource
would be relatively straightforward (adding more terms and operations to the
grammar). Adding support for higher-order resources in general, so that locks
can be defined rather than built-in, would be substantial change to the
\kl{resource type} definitions and rules.

\section{Restrictions on branching}\label{sec:restriction-branching}

As mentioned in~\fancyref{sec:auto-unfold-functions}, the formalisation
distinguishes two features (unfolding definitions, and selecting a branch of an
ordered-disjunction) which are conflated in the implementation. This also
unnecessarily restricts the syntax for predicate definitions: they must either
have no ordered-disjunctions, or only one, placed first at the
top-level.\sidenote{\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/issues/266}{Cerberus\#266}}

With the formalisation, I can explain why that restriction is unnecessary, and
shed light on how to lift it.

Restricting the surface syntax is unnecessary because the syntax is orthogonal
to the internal representation of \kl(CN){predicate definitions}. The key insight
here is that lifting this feature would not add any extra expressive power,
because users can (and do) clunkily work around it by manually defining an
auxiliary \kl(CN){predicate} each time they wish to use an \kl{ordered-disjunction}
anywhere which is not first and at the top-level.

The same orthogonality also gives us options on how to lift the restriction. If
we wanted, we could generalise the surface syntax whilst retaining most of the
implementation internals, by automating the work-around users already use. Or
we could generalise the internals first, and change the surface syntax later,
to ensure a smooth transition. The first option will be quicker but the second
produces less technical debt.

\section{Removing the pointer first restriction on predicates}\label{sec:rm-ptr-first}

Another awkward \kl{CN} restriction is requiring the first argument to a
\kl(CN){predicate} to be a pointer. This is usually fine because it would be
strange to speak of ownership without a pointer, but sometimes the pointer is
derived from elsewhere (a global base pointer, but changing indices), or
sometimes the pointer is in a record and that is the easiest thing to pass
in.\sidenote{\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/issues/303}{Cerberus\#303}.}

With the formalisation, we can see lifting this restriction would hinder
\kl{CN}'s ability to infer indices when handling \kl(CN){iterated predicates}
(\S\ref{subsec:synth-indices}). But, given that \kl{CN} does not infer those
anyway,\sidenote{See note~\ref{sn:new-inf-statics}.} we do not lose automation
or expressiveness.\sidenote{It will not increase expressiveness either, because
users currently can and do just use a dummy parameter.}

\section{Unifying the syntax of functions, predicates and specifications}

Syntax is part of the user-interface of \kl{CN}, and as such it is subject to
strong constraints, strong scrutiny and strong opinions.

Currently, logical \kl(CN){functions} and \kl(CN){predicates} are separated
(represented by different OCaml types) in the implementation. This is with good
reason: the pure parts are shipped off to an SMT solver, but the \kl{heap}
parts are handled by \kl{resource inference}. However, \kl{CN} also enforces
this distinction syntactically, which is clunky and occasionally
confusing,\sidenote{\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/issues/288}{Cerberus\#288}.}.

This could be remedied by unifying the two syntaxes and using another method to
enforcing the distinction between the pure and \kl{heap}
worlds.\sidenote{\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/issues/304}{Cerberus\#304}.}
Exposing the monad implicit in the \kl(CN){predicate} syntax as a type constructor
would achieve both; whilst this is theoretically elegant, it could be too
complicated for users.

\chapter{An alternative presentation}\label{chap:kernel-alternative}

\margintoc{}%
%
I will conclude this part by sketching out an alternative presentation of the
\kl{CN} type system, \intro{MiniCN}, which is closer to both C as a surface
language and the \kl{CN} implementation. It will demonstrate the challenges of
\emph{not} using \kl{ResCore}, because \kl{MiniC} is an imperative,
expression-based language with implicit \kl{resource passing}. The latter means the
inference cannot be specified separately, whereas the former complicates typing
early returns and join-points. It will also demonstrate the challenges which
seem to be inherent to designing a type system for a C-like language.

\section{MiniC and MiniCN}

\kl{MiniC} is a C-like language intended to capture the parts of C relevant to
explaining the core ideas behind
\intro{Fulminate}~\sidecite{banerjee2025fulminate}, a separation-logic testing
framework which translates \kl{CN} specifications to C. It includes global
variables, mutable function parameters, block-scoped local variables, and early
return, but omits loops and unstructured control flow such as \cinline{goto}.
Expressions and statements are unified into a single construct \sm{[[e]]}, and evaluation
is fixed to be left-to-right. The only MiniC types are integers or pointers
(with free conversion between the two), so there is no structured data such as
structs or arrays. Notably, \kl{MiniC} includes taking the address of
variables and dereferencing arbitrary expressions; indirect assignment is also
supported via \sm{[[*lve]]}. Its expression grammar is shown in
\cref{fig:minic-grammar}.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \cngrammarcompressed{\textwidth}{%
        \cndecl{}\cninterrule{}
        \cnblock{}\cninterrule{}
        \cnlve{}\cninterrule{}
        \cne{}\cninterrule{}
        \cncfuncXXdef{}\cnafterlastrule{}
    }%
    \caption{Grammar for a C-like language, \kl{MiniC}.}\label{fig:minic-grammar}
\end{figure*}

Peter Sewell contributed the dynamic semantics of the \kl{MiniC} expression
fragment (Section 3.2 in~\textcite{banerjee2025fulminate}); I contributed the
dynamic semantics of the \kl{MiniC} specification langauge (Section 3.3),
and a proof of correspondence between resource-passing and stack-depth-tracking
versions (Section 3.4) of both semantics. I will omit discussion of that,
focusing here on typing rules for \kl{MiniCN}.

The specification language for \kl{MiniC}, is a simplified version of the
surface syntax of \kl{CN} (\cref{fig:surface-cn-syntax}). The simplifications
are to (a) equate pointers and integers, (b) assume that all
\sm{\cnkw{Owned}}s are always initialised, (c) ignore any other C types,
including structs and arrays, and (d) ignore iterated predicates. Using this, I
define \kl{MiniCN} as the typing rules for \kl{MiniC} using this simplified
specification language.

\section{Aliasing requires linear resources}\label{sec:minicn-aliasing}

Because syntactically different expressions may alias the same heap locations,
the type system still needs symbolic expressions and \kl{linearity} to track
permissions on reading and writing locations (there is no allocation and
destruction). This is visible in the \textsc{Mini\_Prove*} judgement in
\cref{fig:minicn-spine}, which is used to prove a specification holds.

\begin{figure*}[tpb]
    \ContinuedFloat*
    \small%
    \raggedright%
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnMiniXXProve{}}{%
        \cndruleMiniXXProveXXOwned{}
    }
    \caption{Proving an $[[Owned<init, ct>]]$ specification in \kl{MiniCN}.}\label{fig:minicn-spine}
\end{figure*}

The typing context includes: \sm{[[Vc]]} for variables (computational and
logical), a constraint context \sm{[[Phi]]}, and a resource context \sm{[[Rc]]}.
Remember that all values are integers so we do not track types in the \sm{[[Vc]]}
context, nor on any binders. The judgement accumulates contexts \sm{[[Vc']];
[[Phi']]; [[Rc']]} whilst checking specification \sm{[[spec]]}. It produces a
specification value \sm{[[sval]]}, a context for new variables \sm{[[Vc'']]}, a
context for new constraints learned \sm{[[Phi'']]}, a context of used resources
\sm{[[Rc1]]}, and a context for leftover resources \sm{[[Rc2]]}. I chose this more
complex accumulating style to mirror the tail-recursive style of the
specification dynamic semantics.

The rule for checking ownership marks the resource in the context
(\sm{[[ id1 |-> id2 ]]}) as used\sidenote{I marked the resources as used and delete
them at the use site, instead of deleting them along the way, to mirror the
dynamic semantics here too.} if the requested location \sm{[[term]]} is proven to
be equal \sm{[[Rc1 , Phi /\ Phi1 => term = id1]]}. Unlike the system
presented in~\cref{sec:elaboration}, the resource context here is not guaranteed
to be normalised, so this must be done lazily before each call to the SMT
solver.\sidenote{Recall that $[[id |-> _x  * id' |-> _x]]$ implies $[[id != id']]$.}
This is accomplished via the premise
\sm{[[ [ Vc, Vc' ] , Rc , Phi /\ Phi' |- simplify ( cdot , Rc , cdot ) ~> Vc1 , Rc1 , Phi1 ]]},
which says that assuming variables \sm{[[ [Vc, Vc'] ]]}, constraints \sm{[[Phi /\ Phi']]},
and resource context \sm{[[Rc]]}, simplify \sm{[[Rc]]} (expand all determined
conditional resources) to produce new variables \sm{[[Vc1]]},
new constraints \sm{[[Phi1]]}, and updated resources \sm{[[Rc1]]}.

\section{Implicit resource terms intertwine elaboration and typing}\label{sec:minicn-implicit}

\kl{MiniCN} is intended to be closer to the syntax (and implementation) than
\kl{Kernel CN}. This is why I phrased the rules in an \kl{algorithmic}-style in
terms of input and output contexts, rather than a \kl{declarative},
context-splitting style. It also means that there are no \kl{resource
terms} or quantifier instantiations in the syntax.

As such, the rules for proving (and assuming) a specification compute
quantifier instantiations on-the-fly. This is why, in addition to outputting a
modified resource context, the judgement also outputs a specification value
\sm{[[sval]]}, as well as the additional constraints learnt (and additional
variables used). This is best demonstrated in~\cref{fig:minicn-spine-take},
where premise 1 says that \sm{[[return term]]} is the result of proving and
evaluating \sm{[[spec]]}, and premise 2 says that given extra variable \sm{[[id]]},
and an extra constraint \sm{[[id = term]]}, the rest of the specification
\sm{[[spec']]} evaluates to \sm{[[sval]]}.

\begin{figure}[tpbh]
    \ContinuedFloat{}
    \small%
    \raggedright%
    \cnusedrule{\cndruleMiniXXProveXXTake{}}
    \caption{Proving a $[[take id = spec; spec']]$ specification in \kl{MiniCN}.}\label{fig:minicn-spine-take}
\end{figure}

\section{Early-returns intertwine normalising, synthesising and checking}\label{sec:minicn-early}

Because expressions and statements are a single construct in \kl{MiniC}, and
statements include \sm{\cnkw{return}} statements, any expression could
potentially have its evaluation interrupted by a nested early-return.
The typing judgement for expressions needs to take this into account, so in
addition to \kl{synthesising} symbolic values for sub-expressions, it also needs to
thread a postcondition \sm{[[spec]]} for \kl{checking} a return expression
(\cref{fig:minicn-return}).

The judgement expresses the fact that given the context \sm{[[Vc]]; [[Phi]];
[[Rc]]}, and \kl{postcondition} \sm{[[y]] .\: [[spec]]}, the expression \sm{[[e]]}
symbolically evaluates to \sm{[[id1]]}, with new variables \sm{[[Vc1]]}, new
constraints \sm{[[Phi1]]}, and updated resources \sm{[[Rc1]]}. Remember that
\sm{[[id1]]} is a fresh variable and that the \kl{postcondition} \sm{[[spec]]} is
defined with respect to the return value bound to \sm{[[y]]}, hence the constraint
\sm{[[y = id1 ]]} for evaluating \sm{[[spec]]}.

\begin{figure*}[tpb]
    \ContinuedFloat*
    \small%
    \raggedright%
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnMiniXXExpr{}}{%
        \cndruleMiniXXExprXXReturn{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{MiniCN} rule for typing a \kl{MiniC} return
    expression.}\label{fig:minicn-return}
\end{figure*}

For the sake of simplicity, the \kl{synthesised} value of each sub-expression
is represented by a fresh variable and constrained in the output constraint
context. In the case of a return expression, the fresh variable remains
unconstrained and the output context has `\sm{\mathsf{false}}' added to it, to
allow any ancestor expression to continue typing correctly.

\begin{figure*}[tpb]
    \ContinuedFloat{}
    \small%
    \raggedright%
    \cnusedrule{\cndruleMiniXXExprXXBinop{}}
    \caption{\kl{MiniCN} rule for typing a \kl{MiniC} a binary operation
    expression.}\label{fig:minicn-bop}
\end{figure*}

In the case of a binary operation expression (\cref{fig:minicn-bop}), this
scheme recovers in the constraint context the \kl{let-normalisation} absent in the
grammar.\sidenote{Remember that the implementation this by passing around
explicit continuations
(\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/commit/350fefc675626dcc69c7adc9edea30ff9687b752}{commit
350fefc6}). Another way of implementing this would be to have the type
system's symbolic evaluation \emph{itself} specified as a non-determinism
monad
(\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/issues/730}{Cerberus\#730}).}

\section{Lack of let-normalisation requires join-points}\label{sec:lack-let-norm}

Aside from handling early-returns, avoiding \kl{let-normalisation} also necessitates
constructing join-points for resource and constraint contexts
(\cref{fig:minicn-if}).

\begin{figure}[tpbh]
    \ContinuedFloat{}
    \small%
    \raggedright%
    \cnusedrule{\cndruleMiniXXExprXXIf{}}
    \caption{\kl{MiniCN} rule for typing a \kl{MiniC}
    if-expression.}\label{fig:minicn-if}
\end{figure}

I chose to represent these join-points as an ordered-disjunction of
\emph{contexts}, both resource and constraint. This is not very satisfying,
because it complicates how to specify a lookup in the resource context. One
possibility, is a scheme where such ordered-disjunctions would be simplified if
either the condition or its negation could be proven, and otherwise require a
syntactic match on both branches (\S\ref{sec:join-points}).

\section{Discussion}

As I showed in the previous sections, the typing judgements for \kl{MiniCN}
become substantially more complex in the absence of key features of
\kl{ResCore}. This would extend to the proof of soundness too, because the
input and output contexts would not remain the same as an expression evaluates.

For concision, I will write \sm{\Omega} to represent all the contexts combined.
For typing a binary operation, \sm{\Omega_0 \vdash e_1 + e_2 \dashv \Omega_2}, the
premises would be \sm{\Omega_0 \vdash e_1 \dashv \Omega_1} and \sm{\Omega_1 \vdash e_2
\dashv \Omega_2}.\sidenote{The rules I presented are not exactly in this format,
outputting a delta of contexts rather than new ones. I believe
the basic approach remains the same.}

Suppose then that \sm{e_1 \rightarrow e_1'}. To prove that \sm{e_1' + e_2} is
well-typed, we would have to show \sm{\Omega_0' \vdash e_1' \dashv \Omega_1'} and
\sm{\Omega_1' \vdash e_2 \dashv \Omega_2'}. This uses different contexts to the
\sm{\Omega_0}, \sm{\Omega_1} and \sm{\Omega_2} in the initial well-typed assumption.  To
strengthen the induction hypothesis, one would need to define a relationship
\sm{\sqsupseteq} between contexts, along with a weakening lemma which stated that
if \sm{\Omega_A \sqsupseteq \Omega_A'} and \sm{\Omega_A \vdash e \dashv \Omega_B} then
\sm{\Omega_A' \vdash e \dashv \Omega_B'} where \sm{\Omega_B \sqsupseteq \Omega_B'}. % chktex 23
% We would then need to apply this to the inductive cases, by demonstrating
% $\Omega_0 \sqsupseteq \Omega_0'$ and $\Omega_1 \sqsupseteq \Omega_1'$.

This resembles a rule of consequence proof for a Hoare-triple, except that one
would need to prove there is a valid \kl{pre-} and \kl{postcondition} for every
step of the evaluation of the program.
